Package: xfilter
Architecture: all
Depends: python3 (>= 3.9),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: openssh-client,
            python3-gi,
            gir1.2-gtk-3.0
Description: policy-enforcing X11 proxy for untrusted clients
 xfilter sits between an X client and the real X server and puts every
 request to a default-deny policy before it is forwarded.  A request is
 passed only if it is on the safe allowlist or a rule decides on it: reads
 of other clients' data are refused, the handful of foreign reads that
 theming and window management need are kept, CLIPBOARD and PRIMARY are
 gated behind a prompt, and anything nobody thought of is blocked and
 logged rather than passed on trust.
 .
 It is meant for the machine with the real X server -- the one you sit at.
 Nothing is installed on the remote host, which is the point: an X client
 reached over "ssh -X" with trusted forwarding can otherwise log your
 keystrokes, read your clipboard and screenshot your desktop, and the
 remote side gets no say in the filtering.
 .
 The proxy is Python 3 standard library only.  It reads the authority
 cookie and the clipboard itself, so neither xauth nor xclip is needed;
 python3-gi and the GTK 3 typelib are needed only for the "--gate ask"
 prompt, which draws on your real display.
